    ["compressed_files",
    ".LBR",
    "LU Library Archive",
    "3.3",
    "An LBR file is an archive created by the LU archiving utility. It contains one or more files that have been packaged together for easier storage and distribution. Notably, LBR files are not compressed. Developer Gary P. Novosielski created LU to help developers more efficiently package and distribute software for the CP/M operating system. Later, LU was also used to package and distribute DOS software. Associated programs, including NULU and LRUN, also allowed users to create and manipulate LBR files. Like.TAR files, LBR files are not compressed. To reduce LBR files size, users often compressed them using Squeeze or Crunch compression. These compressed LBR files often use different file extensions. For example, LBR files compressed using Squeeze use the.LQR extension, and LBR files compressed using Crunch use the.LZR extension. NOTE: LBR is an abbreviation for library.. You can use CFX (Windows), The Unarchiver (Mac), lbrate (Linux), LAR (Linux), or to extract the files an LBR file contains. Windows Peter Dassow CFX. Mac The Unarchiver. Linux lbrate. LARFree.",
    "Windows Peter Dassow CFX. Mac The Unarchiver. Linux lbrate. LAR.",
    "?"],
    ["compressed_files",
    ".ZOO",
    "Zoo Compressed File",
    "3.2",
    "A ZOO file is a compressed archive created by the zoo compression program. It contains one or more files that have been compressed using a variant of the Lempel-Ziv-Welch (LZW) compression algorithm. Zoo was primarily available on Unix systems, so Unix users are most likely to encounter ZOO files. In the 1980s, developer Rahul Dhesi created the zoo compression program and the ZOO file format. Zoo was available for Unix-based operating systems, MS-DOS, and AmigaOS. Notably, ZOO archives can contain multiple versions of the same file, if each version was modified on a different date. This allows zoo users to save and retrieve prior versions of a file from a ZOO archive, to revert to a previous version or compare an old version to a more recent version. You can open a ZOO file with Logipole Konvertor (Windows), ESTsoft ALZip (Windows), The Unarchiver (Mac), or zoo (Windows, Linux). Windows zoo. Logipole Konvertor. ESTsoft ALZip. Mac The Unarchiver. Linux zooFree. Saved game file created with Zoo Tycoon, a zoo simulation game developed by Microsoft; stores game progress so the player can resume the game at a later time; should be stored in the Saved Games folder within the Zoo Tycoon directory. Windows Microsoft Zoo Tycoon.",
    "Windows zoo. Logipole Konvertor. ESTsoft ALZip. Mac The Unarchiver. Linux zoo. Windows Microsoft Zoo Tycoon.",
    "?"],
    ["compressed_files",
    ".SHR",
    "Unix Shell Archive File",
    "3.2",
    "Self-extracting archive created with the Unix shar program; contains a shell script with instructions on how to extract the contents; used on Unix systems with the Bourne shell installed. Shar archives also use the.SH and.SHAR extensions. Windows ZipZag Linux unshar.",
    "Windows ZipZag. Linux unshar.",
    "?"],
    ["compressed_files",
    ".PET",
    "Puppy Linux Install Package",
    "3.2",
    "A PET file is a 3D model file used by WangReal Engine games, such as Pangya and White Day: A Labyrinth Named School. It contains a 3D model, which includes texture, bone, animation, mesh, and facial animation information. PET files are distributed with WangReal Engine games and are compressed in.NOP and.PAK archives. You will most likely only need to open a PET file if you are looking to modify a character in the game. You can open PET files with Pangya Models View in Windows. You can also import PET files in 3DSMax and Blender with the 3DSMax add-on and Blender add-on. White Day: A Labyrinth Named School PET files are packaged in NOP archives and Pangya PET files are packaged in PAK archives. The archives can be found in the directory in which the game is installed. Windows Sonnori White Day: A Labyrinth Named School. Application package created for PuppyLinux; typically used for installing software programs; saved in a stored in a tarball compressed archive format (.TGZ files) with an md5sum checksum appended to the end. PET stands for Puppys Extra Treats and is a unified package system used by Puppy Linux 2.14 and later. PET files can be opened by the included PETget utility, or with any TGZ file extractor. When extracted, the packages directory structure is projected onto the root level of the Linux file system. PET packages are commonly referred to as pets. They replace.PUP files used by earlier versions of Puppy Linux. Windows Smith Micro Stuffit Deluxe. Corel WinZip. RARLAB WinRAR. 7-zip. Mac Smith Micro StuffIt Deluxe Mac 16. Linux PETget. tar.",
    "Windows Sonnori White Day: A Labyrinth Named School. Windows Smith Micro Stuffit Deluxe. Corel WinZip. RARLAB WinRAR. 7-zip. Mac Smith Micro StuffIt Deluxe Mac 16. Linux PETget. tar.",
    "?"],
    ["compressed_files",
    ".IADPROJ",
    "iAd Producer Project",
    "3.2",
    "Project archive format created and used by iAd Producer, an application used to design and assemble rich interactive content for iOS advertisements; contains files that make up an iAd; includes assets, banners, code, pages, styles, user data, layers, and metadata. The content created with iAd Producer is distributed through Apples iAd network within iOS apps. You can create content for different iPhone and iPad screen sizes. The program supports the insertion of text, images, video, and audio and also allows you to apply animations and styles to different objects in your content. iAd Producer was introduced by Apple in 2010. Mac Apple iAd Producer —  (Discontinued).",
    "Mac Apple iAd Producer —  (Discontinued).",
    "?"],
    ["compressed_files",
    ".SAR",
    "SAPCAR Archive",
    "3.2",
    "Stores musical arrangements used by Sibelius music notation software; allows songs with similar styles to be composed from the same basic configuration; may include instruments, track configurations, meter, tempo, and other settings. Windows Sibelius music notation software Mac Sibelius music notation software. A SAR file is a compressed archive created by the SAPCAR compression utility. It contains one or more files used by the SAP enterprise resource planning (ERP) suite. SAR files usually contain SAP executables. SAP is an ERP suite that allows businesses to centralize their data and business logic. Versions 4.6C and above of SAP include a proprietary compression utility named SAPCAR. (In previous versions, this utility was named just CAR.) SAPCAR creates and can extract files from compressed archives saved as SAR files. Often, IT administrators provide SAR files that contain additional or updated programs to end-users, so those users can install the programs on their machine. You can extract the files a SAR archive contains using the SAPCAR utility (Windows, Linux). Mac SAP SAPCAR. Linux SAP SAPCAR. Graphic created with Saracen Paint, an image creation application for Commodore 64/128 systems; contains a color bitmap image; can be converted to more modern image formats using some image converter programs. NOTE: Commodore 128 systems were discontinued in 1989. Windows Newera Graphics Converter Pro. XnViewMP. Mac XnViewMP. Linux XnViewMPFree.",
    "Windows Sibelius music notation software. Mac Sibelius music notation software. Mac SAP SAPCAR. Linux SAP SAPCAR. Windows Newera Graphics Converter Pro. XnViewMP. Mac XnViewMP. Linux XnViewMP.",
    "?"],
